{{also|miliția}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|mīlitia||army, military force/service}}, from {{m|la|mīles||soldier}}. {{doublet|en|militsia}}.

The use of "militia" rather than "police" to refer to the police force (of Belarus and some other countries) originated in the [[USSR]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/məˈlɪʃə/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-militia.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪʃə|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|militiae}}

# An [[army]] of [[train]]ed [[civilian]]s, which may be an [[official]] [[reserve]] army, called upon in time of need, the entire [[able-bodied]] [[population]] of a state which may also be called upon, or a [[private]] force not under [[government]] [[control]].
# {{synonym of|en|militsia}}: the national [[police force]] of certain countries (e.g. Belarus).
[[File:Militia 1779 cartoon.png|thumb|A militia.]]
